<%# locals: subscription:

    What "Not working" or "Wrong address" means and what to do about it —
    nothing at all when the subscription is fine. Paired with
    `_status_pill`, and shared by the same two screens for the same reason. %>
<% if subscription.broken? %>
  <%# Two ways to end up here, and only one of them is the reader's to fix.
      A credential refusal is undone by signing in again — including from this
      state, which it used to be unable to reach. Anything else needs the
      repository added afresh. %>
  <%# The reason is self-contained on purpose — it says what refused us, when
      the run started and how many attempts it took, because the delivery log
      that would otherwise answer that is pruned at a fortnight and we give up
      at thirty days. See WebhookSubscription#give_up_reason. %>
  <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-removed" data-testid="broken-reason">
    <%= subscription.broken_reason %>
    <% if subscription.revivable_by_credential? %>
      Signing in to Prism again starts it watching from scratch, and it will go back over
      the pull requests it missed.
    <% else %>
      Stop watching this repository and add it again to start over.
    <% end %>
  </p>
<% elsif subscription.callback_stale? %>
  <%# Silent failure otherwise: GitHub keeps POSTing to an address nothing
      answers on, and because the request never reaches Prism there is
      nothing in any log here to find. %>
  <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-modified" data-testid="stale-reason">
    GitHub is still delivering to <code class="font-mono text-xs break-all"><%= subscription.callback_url %></code>,
    but Prism now answers on <code class="font-mono text-xs break-all"><%= Webhooks::CallbackUrl.current_or_nil %></code>.
    Nothing is arriving. Re-register to point the existing webhook at the new address —
    the secret and history are kept.
  </p>
<% elsif subscription.suspended? %>
  <%# Deliberately undramatic, and it asks for nothing. This state fixes
      itself: Prism retries on a schedule as well as on the next delivery, so
      it no longer depends on the repository being busy enough to produce one.
      Telling people to "remove and re-add the repository" — which the old
      copy did, for a state that recovered on its own — sent them to do work
      that was never needed. %>
  <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-ink-soft" data-testid="suspended-reason">
    <%= subscription.broken_reason %> Prism keeps retrying in the background — on the next
    pull request in this repository, and every couple of hours regardless — and signing in
    to Prism again fixes it straight away. If you have signed in since, it is already sorted.
  </p>
<% end %>
